{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And your seed shall be as the dust of the earth, and you shall break forth.</b> It should have said וְיִפְרְצוּ — 'and they shall break forth.' It is an allusion to what our Rabbis of blessed memory said in the Midrash (Lamentations Rabbah 2:7): when evil comes, none feels it but Jacob, as it says (Lamentations 2:3), ״וַיִּבְעַר בְּיַעֲקֹב כְּאֵשׁ לֶהָבָה״, and when the good comes, none feels it but Jacob, as it says (Psalms 14:7), ״בְּשׁוּב ה׳ אֶת שְׁבוּת עַמּוֹ יָגֵל יַעֲקֹב יִשְׂמַח יִשְׂרָאֵל״. Therefore it says ״וּפָרַצְתָּ״ — for since Jacob alone feels their good, it is as though he himself is the one who breaks forth." "And as for the matter of 'the dust of the earth,' it appears that this prophecy came concerning the Egyptian exile, for at that time our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sotah 11a) that the Egyptians plowed upon them, as it says (Psalms 129:3), ״עַל גַּבִּי חָרְשׁוּ חֹרְשִׁים״, and they would come home in flocks upon flocks, and so forth. It follows that the cause of their multiplying was that they were like the dust of the earth upon which people plow — just as they plowed upon it, so they plowed upon them; and through this cause they multiplied exceedingly in the midst of the land literally. And they were all so numerous that there were enough among them to be divided into four standards, one for each direction; therefore it says ״וּפָרַצְתָּ יָמָּה וָקֵדְמָה״ and so forth. And as it is written (Numbers 23:10), ״מִי מָנָה עֲפַר יַעֲקֹב וּמִסְפָּר אֶת רֹבַע יִשְׂרָאֵל״, and its Targum is ״מֵאַרְבַּע מַשְׁרְיָתָא״ — 'from four camps' — to tell you that they merited four standards by virtue of Jacob being like this dust upon which all plow." "Another thing: ״וְהָיָה זַרְעֲךָ כַּעֲפַר״ — in the merit of affliction they shall be like this dust upon which all trample, and then they shall break forth and multiply. For whoever was in affliction — 'lest he multiply' — was in reward 'so shall he multiply.' Another thing: in the merit of the humility of Abraham, who said (Genesis 18:27), ״וְאָנֹכִי עָפָר וָאֵפֶר״, they shall merit to break forth to all four winds, as it is written (Psalms 37:11), ״וַעֲנָוִים יִירְשׁוּ אָרֶץ״. Another thing: because it is said concerning those who keep Shabbat, ״וְהַאֲכַלְתִּיךָ נַחֲלַת יַעֲקֹב אָבִיךָ״ (Isaiah 58:14) — that is, an inheritance without constraint, as mentioned here — therefore He said that your seed shall be as the dust of the earth. For just as the earth needs to rest, as it says, ״וְשָׁבְתָה הָאָרֶץ שַׁבָּת לַה׳״, so too your seed shall rest on the seventh day, and in this merit they shall break forth westward and eastward and so forth." "<b>And you shall break forth westward and eastward, northward and southward.</b> The reason He mentioned the directions as opposites, paired one against the other, and did not mention them in their normal order, is because they said in the Midrash (see Midrash Tehillim 44:2) that salvation does not come to Israel except at a time when they are in the utmost lowliness, as it says (Psalms 44:26), ״כִּי שָׁחָה לֶעָפָר נַפְשֵׁנוּ״ and so forth — what is written after it? ״קוּמָה עֶזְרָתָה לָּנוּ״. And the reason for this is that as long as they are not in the utmost lowliness, they do not hang their trust in HaShem, but rather devise stratagems to escape the snare through human contrivances; therefore HaShem keeps His distance from man and the abandonment is great, because they did not trust in the salvation of HaShem. But when they see that all their efforts have failed and there is no helper or supporter, then their eyes are lifted to HaShem to call upon Him in their time of distress, and HaShem hears — and at that time salvation comes by way of a flight from one extreme to the other, that is, from the utmost lowliness to the utmost exaltation.\n\nAnd since according to nature it would not be believed that they could rise from the lowliness of their station to the height of a dwelling-place, therefore it was said to Jacob in this vision three ascending degrees, each in the manner of לֹא זוֹ אַף זוֹ — 'not only this, but even that.' For first He said, ״וְהָיָה זַרְעֲךָ כַּעֲפַר הָאָרֶץ״ — at the time when their soul is bowed down to the dust; at that time ״וּפָרַצְתָּ״, meaning that the One who breaks through shall go up before them to raise them to the rung of exaltation.\n\nAnd if you should say: even if they rise from lowliness to some degree of greatness, in any case it is impossible that they should flee from one extreme to the other to be in a great degree, but rather at their stumbling they are helped with a small measure of help, as in the prophecy of Daniel (Daniel 11:34) — Scripture comes to teach ״יָמָּה וָקֵדְמָה״, for these two directions are opposites and mutually inverse, for in the east the sun always rises and in the west it always sets. And after that — in the time when the sun of Israel is setting, as Rashi explained on the verse ״וְלָעֶרֶב יְחַלֵּק שָׁלָל״ (Genesis 49:27) — at that time of setting they shall flee to the opposite extreme, so that their throne shall be as the sun opposite Him, blessed be He.\n\nAnd if you should say that this will only be so if they are like the west wind — for even though the sun sets there, in any case it also has some rising in that direction, and so too Israel, at a time when they have a little rising from there, shall ascend to be like the east Wind — yet even there the sun does not always shine, as during the season of Tevet, and so their dominion will not be constant — Scripture comes to teach ״צָפֹנָה וָנֶגְבָּה״: for even if they are like the north Wind, concerning which it is said ״צַר וָאוֹר חָשַׁךְ בַּעֲרִיפֶיהָ״ (Isaiah 5:30), in which there is no light at all, and so Israel would be utterly devoid of light — from there they shall ascend to the last extreme, the one opposite it, to be like the south Wind, which is called נֶגֶב on account of ״דָּר רוּם״, for the sun always shines there and dwells in its height; so too the shining of Israel's sun shall be constant forever, as it is written (Isaiah 60:20), ״לֹא יָבוֹא עוֹד שִׁמְשֵׁךְ״, and they shall dwell forever in the height of the world. And this is the promise concerning the final redemption."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:31:15.140656933Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}